UID:a4-residency-chengdu-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261027T140941Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261231
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20270101
SUMMARY:Deadline: A4 Residency Art Center: International Residency Fellowsh
 ip 2028 (Chengdu)
DESCRIPTION:The A4 Residency Art Center in Chengdu\, the residency platform
  of the A4 Art Museum in Luxelakes Eco-City\, opens its full-year 2028 rec
 ruitment. The interdisciplinary\, open and collaborative platform fosters 
 creative exchange among artists\, scientists\, writers and other professio
 nals\, with residents initiating participatory local activities that conne
 ct them with the city and community\; A4 also organises group activities\,
  sharing sessions\, media promotion and exhibition opportunities. CYCLES: 
 Spring 2028 (April to June) and Autumn 2028 (July to October)\, each appro
 ximately eight weeks. WHAT YOU GET (International Residency Fellowship\, f
 ully funded): private studio and accommodation\, round-trip economy airfar
 e or second-class train within a set budget\, a production grant of RMB 10
 \,000 per person or team for materials and production (excluding living ex
 penses)\, community engagement and cultural experiences\, media promotion 
 and networking. ELIGIBILITY: multidisciplinary creators worldwide\, includ
 ing visual and performing artists\, musicians\, filmmakers\, architects\, 
 curators\, designers and creatives with interdisciplinary backgrounds brid
 ging art\, business and culture. APPLICATION: free\; submit a photo\, port
 folio\, CV and project proposal in English and Chinese to a4residency@a4am
 .cn\, with the subject line specifying the programme tier and preferred cy
 cle. DEADLINE: 31 December 2026 for the 2028 full-year recruitment. A para
 llel self-funded light track exists\; this listing covers the funded fello
 wship only.\n\nOrganisation: A4 Residency Art Center (A4 Art Museum)\nLoca
 tion: Luxelakes Eco-City\, Tianfu New Area\, Chengdu\, China. Open worldwi
 de.\nAward: Fully funded fellowship: private studio and accommodation\, ro
 und-trip economy airfare or second-class train ticket within a set budget\
 , and a production grant of RMB 10\,000 per person/team for materials and 
 production (excluding living expenses). No application fee.\nDuration: App
 roximately 8 weeks\, in the Spring 2028 (April to June) or Autumn 2028 (Ju
 ly to October) cycle.\n\nMore: https://www.a4artmuseum.com/en/a4-residency
 artcenter/residing/latest-recruitment/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantde
 sk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.a4artmuseum.com/en/a4-residencyartcenter/residing/latest-re
 cruitment/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Luxelakes Eco-City\, Tianfu New Area\, Chengdu\, China. Open world
 wide.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:culture360-asef-arts-in-the-wake-of-ai-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261027T140941Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260929
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260930
SUMMARY:Deadline: culture360 / ASEF: Call for Content\, Arts in the Wake of
  AI
DESCRIPTION:culture360.ASEF.org\, the arts platform of the Asia-Europe Foun
 dation\, invites proposals from writers and content producers for its open
  call 'Arts in the Wake of AI': articles\, podcasts and video interviews t
 hat foreground how cultural professionals and organisations across Asia an
 d Europe are adapting to AI technologies\, from generative tools and machi
 ne learning to recommender systems and algorithms. WHAT THEY WANT: case-st
 udy-based contributions grounded in real-world evidence\, experiences or p
 ractices\, on topics including the impact of AI on cultural diversity\, fa
 irness and inclusivity\; the environmental costs of AI in the cultural sec
 tor\; and AI governance in the cultural sector (policy\, copyright\, intel
 lectual property and the ethical use of AI-generated cultural content). Ac
 ademic research papers and non-arts subjects are not considered. FEE: SGD 
 250 per commissioned article\, podcast or video interview. FORMATS: journa
 listic articles of 800 to 1\,000 words with at least four credited images\
 ; podcasts and video interviews of 15 to 20 minutes. ELIGIBILITY: national
 s of ASEM partner countries with previous experience in writing\, podcasti
 ng or video production\, proficiency in English\, and knowledge of or invo
 lvement in the Asian and European arts and culture scene\; an existing cha
 nnel for further dissemination is an advantage\; the work is fully virtual
 . Up to two proposals per application. Only selected writers are notified.
  Queries to culture[at]asef.org. Deadline 29 September 2026 at 6 PM Singap
 ore time\, via the online application form.\n\nOrganisation: Asia-Europe F
 oundation (ASEF)\, culture360.ASEF.org\nLocation: Fully remote\; contribut
 ors do not need to be based in Singapore. Open to nationals of ASEM partne
 r countries across Asia and Europe.\nAward: SGD 250 fee per commissioned a
 rticle\, podcast or video interview. Up to two proposals per application\,
  of which at most one is selected. No application fee.\nDuration: One-off 
 editorial commissions: articles of 800 to 1\,000 words with at least four 
 credited pictures\, podcasts and video interviews of 15 to 20 minutes\n\nM
 ore: https://culture360.asef.org/opportunities/call-for-content-arts-ai/?u
 tm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://culture360.asef.org/opportunities/call-for-content-arts-ai/?utm
 _source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Fully remote\; contributors do not need to be based in Singapore. 
 Open to nationals of ASEM partner countries across Asia and Europe.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:pulitzer-center-global-south-civil-society-microgrants-2026@artificialn
 ouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261027T140941Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260906
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260907
SUMMARY:Deadline: Pulitzer Center: Global South Civil Society Microgrants 2
 026
DESCRIPTION:The Pulitzer Center funds civil society in the Global South to 
 build civic engagement projects around Pulitzer Center-supported journalis
 m: grassroots collectives\, community-led movements\, ARTISTS and civic co
 alitions taking published reporting to the communities it concerns. This g
 rant cycle is restricted to the Center's Climate and Environment topics: R
 AINFOREST (drivers of rainforest degradation\, the role of global markets\
 , finance and policy\, centring the rights and roles of Indigenous peoples
 )\, OCEAN (degradation of marine environments\, ocean health and the clima
 te crisis\, ocean governance\, science-backed and community-led solutions)
 \, and CLIMATE AND LABOR (the intersection of climate and labour\, includi
 ng gender\, livelihoods\, adaptation and community resilience). WHAT COUNT
 S AS A PROJECT: community engagement and organising that connects reportin
 g with audiences\; creative expressions\, explicitly including digital cam
 paigns\, public performances and creative publication formats\; public dia
 logues bringing together affected communities\, journalists\, decision-mak
 ers and academia\; and other innovative uses of the reporting that build o
 n local cultural practices. Every project must use at least one published 
 Pulitzer Center-supported reporting project meaningfully\, or involve a Pu
 litzer Center-supported journalist centrally\, and must name a clear targe
 t audience. OUT OF SCOPE: journalism and reporting projects themselves (th
 ose go to the Center's reporting grants)\, anything not built on Pulitzer 
 Center-supported reporting\, direct advocacy or lobbying\, campaigns endor
 sing political candidates or parties\, and for-profit projects. WHO CAN AP
 PLY: grassroots organisations\, community educators\, civil society organi
 sations and coalitions\, youth movements and other groups working at the i
 ntersection of journalism\, civic rights and active citizenship\, with pas
 t experience running civic engagement activities in the Global South\, pro
 ven experience on the named topics\, and the operational capacity to manag
 e a microgrant from an international organisation. APPLICATION: a project 
 description of no more than 400 words\, the target audience and desired im
 pact\, your methodology\, a timeline and budget\, the lead applicant's CV 
 with a letter of recommendation\, and\, where activities involve Indigenou
 s peoples or traditional communities\, a signed or recorded statement of c
 onsent from a community member. Applications are accepted in English\, Spa
 nish\, Portuguese\, French and Indonesian through Submittable. TIMELINE: a
 pplications 15 August to 6 September 2026\, selection announced mid-Septem
 ber 2026\, onboarding October 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Pulitzer Center\nLoca
 tion: Global South\, with the call naming Africa\, Latin America and South
 east Asia. Groups based in the United States apply to the separate U.S. Ci
 vil Society Microgrant instead.\nAward: USD 2\,000 to USD 4\,000 per proje
 ct. Applicants name the amount requested and submit a draft budget. Funds 
 are transferred to the partner after the agreement is signed\; partners fi
 le a financial narrative and outcomes report at the end but are NOT requir
 ed to collect detailed receipts. No application fee.\nDuration: Up to six 
 months of implementation\, October 2026 to March 2027\, with a virtual che
 ck-in in December 2026 and closure in early March 2027.\n\nMore: https://p
 ulitzercenter.org/opportunities/reporting-grants-fellowships/global-south-
 civil-society-microgrants?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium
 =referral
URL:https://pulitzercenter.org/opportunities/reporting-grants-fellowships/g
 lobal-south-civil-society-microgrants?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdes
 k&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Global South\, with the call naming Africa\, Latin America and Sou
 theast Asia. Groups based in the United States apply to the separate U.S. 
 Civil Society Microgrant instead.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:design-trust-seed-feature-impact-grants-2026@artificialnouveau.github.i
 o
DTSTAMP:20261027T140941Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261020
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261021
SUMMARY:Deadline: Design Trust Seed\, Feature & Impact Grants (Hong Kong / 
 Greater Bay Area)
DESCRIPTION:Design Trust offers grants to individual designers\, curators\,
  collectives\, and non-profit organisations for projects and activities re
 levant to various design disciplines\, supporting work relevant to the con
 text and content of Hong Kong and the Greater Bay Area (the Special Admini
 strative Regions of Hong Kong and Macao and the nine Guangdong municipalit
 ies of Guangzhou\, Shenzhen\, Zhuhai\, Foshan\, Huizhou\, Dongguan\, Zhong
 shan\, Jiangmen\, and Zhaoqing). It supports innovative\, thought-provokin
 g investigations across design disciplines from graphics\, media\, and wea
 rables to architecture and the built environment\, including exhibitions\,
  research projects\, and creative installations that advocate the positive
  role of design in the region. THREE GRANTS: (1) SEED GRANT (HKD 50\,000) 
 fosters experimentation\, testing\, and sharing\, awarded to individuals k
 ick-starting a meaningful project with social\, educational\, economical\,
  or environmental impact\; supports emerging designers\, young scholars\, 
 and projects in the pipeline needing additional resources\, including rese
 arch and prototypes focused on Hong Kong and Greater Bay Area issues to be
  showcased at international venues\, symposia\, or biennales. (2) FEATURE 
 GRANT (capped at HKD 300\,000\, awarded annually upon special consideratio
 n) fosters cross-disciplinary projects on the Greater Bay Area with wide-r
 eaching audiences\, often sited at an exhibition or biennale of internatio
 nal standing\, supporting exchange between a Hong Kong/China-based collect
 ive and an internationally renowned cultural institution. (3) IMPACT GRANT
  supports public-facing\, community-based projects led by former Design Tr
 ust grant recipients\, fellows\, and mentees with a proven track record\; 
 considered case-by-case (contact grantapplication@designtrust.hk). ELIGIBI
 LITY: individuals\, collectives\, and certified non-profit organisations e
 ither based in the Greater Bay Area or seeking support for design projects
  related to the region. Individuals or collectives who have completed two 
 Feature Grants or three Seed Grants should apply for the Impact Grant inst
 ead. APPLICATION: two cycles annually\, deadlines 20 April and 20 October 
 (late submissions roll to the next cycle)\; apply at least 3 to 6 months b
 efore the project's commencement. Design Trust may provide full or partial
  funding on a case-by-case basis.\n\nOrganisation: Design Trust\nLocation:
  Hong Kong and the Greater Bay Area. Open to applicants based in the regio
 n or seeking support for design projects related to it\, including interna
 tional applicants.\nAward: Seed Grant: HKD 50\,000. Feature Grant: capped 
 at HKD 300\,000\, awarded annually upon special consideration. Impact Gran
 t: case-by-case\, for former grantees. Full or partial funding on a case-b
 y-case basis. Free to apply.\nDuration: Two grant cycles annually (deadlin
 es 20 April and 20 October)\; apply 3 to 6 months before project commencem
 ent\n\nMore: https://designtrust.hk/grants/dtgrants/?utm_source=artificial
 nouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://designtrust.hk/grants/dtgrants/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugra
 ntdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Hong Kong and the Greater Bay Area. Open to applicants based in th
 e region or seeking support for design projects related to it\, including 
 international applicants.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:peripheral-acts-edition-2-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261027T140941Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261004
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261005
SUMMARY:Deadline: Peripheral Acts Edition 2: Leaning Out (UAE balcony resid
 ency)
DESCRIPTION:Peripheral Acts is an experimental\, artist-led residency progr
 amme supporting artistic production alongside dialogue\, exchange and site
 -responsive experimentation\, rooted in everyday urban life in the UAE. Th
 e second edition\, Leaning Out\, takes residential balconies as its site: 
 suspended between private life and public view\, the balcony becomes a sta
 ge for negotiations between host and guest\, inhabitant and observer\, fra
 ming questions of privacy\, voyeurism\, belonging and ownership. STRUCTURE
 : three commissioned artists take part in two phases. Phase 1 is four in-p
 erson gatherings hosted by residency programmer Shazia Salam in Dubai and 
 Abu Dhabi (1\, 7\, 14 and 21 November 2026\, roughly two hours each\; atte
 ndance at all four is expected). Phase 2 is a one-month balcony takeover p
 er artist (January\, February or March 2027)\, during which each resident 
 also hosts one (semi-)public event sharing their practice and process. The
 re is no fixed formula for what can happen on the site\; interventions are
  shaped by its conditions\, permissions and chance encounters\, carefully 
 testing the possibilities and boundaries of informal public intervention i
 n the UAE. FUNDING: AED 1\,000 commission fee plus AED 1\,500 production b
 udget per artist. ELIGIBILITY: artists currently based in the UAE with eas
 y\, ongoing access to a residential balcony (their own or one belonging to
  a friend or family member). Questions: shaziasalam.92@gmail.com. APPLY vi
 a the Google Form. DEADLINE: 4 October 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Peripheral A
 cts\, conceptualised and hosted by Shazia Salam and Priyanka Mehra\nLocati
 on: Dubai and Abu Dhabi\, UAE. Open to artists currently based in the UAE 
 with easy\, ongoing access to a residential balcony (their own\, or a frie
 nd's or family member's).\nAward: AED 1\,000 artist commission fee plus AE
 D 1\,500 production budget per artist. Three artists commissioned. No appl
 ication fee.\nDuration: Phase 1: four in-person gatherings in Dubai and Ab
 u Dhabi on 1\, 7\, 14 and 21 November 2026 (approx. 2 hours each). Phase 2
 : one-month balcony takeovers\, January\, February or March 2027 (one arti
 st per month).\n\nMore: https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLScVzoHsSO
 bapDUhXkeK02Ya1JpxzQH3QjVRtvhfoWDM-KWeSA/viewform?utm_source=artificialnou
 veaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLScVzoHsSObapDUhXkeK02Ya1JpxzQH
 3QjVRtvhfoWDM-KWeSA/viewform?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:Dubai and Abu Dhabi\, UAE. Open to artists currently based in the 
 UAE with easy\, ongoing access to a residential balcony (their own\, or a 
 friend's or family member's).
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:alternative-space-loop-open-call-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261027T140941Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261005
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261006
SUMMARY:Deadline: 2027 LOOP Open Call for Exhibition Proposals (Seoul)
DESCRIPTION:Alternative Space LOOP in Seoul invites proposals for exhibitio
 ns to be presented as part of its 2027 programme. LOOP no longer separates
  artists\, curators and other types of applicants: proposals are welcome f
 rom individuals and teams\, across all genres and media\, and in any exhib
 ition format\, including solo exhibitions\, group exhibitions and curatori
 al projects. LOOP looks for projects that explore new approaches to exhibi
 tion-making and reflect on the social role of contemporary art. SUPPORT: a
  project grant of KRW 1\,000\,000\, exhibition space at Alternative Space 
 LOOP\, curatorial and operational support\, exhibition graphic design and 
 promotion\, use of LOOP's available equipment\, and connections to LOOP's 
 future projects and its local and international networks. SELECTION: 1-2 i
 ndividuals or teams\; interviews with shortlisted applicants mid October 2
 026\, final selection announced late October 2026. HOW TO APPLY: download 
 the application form via the Google Form\, complete it\, and upload it to 
 the same form as a PDF of no more than 25 pages\, named '2027 LOOP Open Ca
 ll_Applicant Name.pdf'. DEADLINE: 5 October 2026 at 24:00 KST.\n\nOrganisa
 tion: Alternative Space LOOP\nLocation: Alternative Space LOOP\, Seoul\, S
 outh Korea. Open to individuals and teams across all genres and media\; no
  separation between artists\, curators and other applicants.\nAward: Proje
 ct grant of KRW 1\,000\,000\, plus exhibition space at Alternative Space L
 OOP\, curatorial and operational support\, exhibition graphic design and p
 romotion\, and use of LOOP's available equipment. 1-2 individuals or teams
  are selected. No application fee mentioned.\nDuration: Exhibition realise
 d as part of LOOP's 2027 programme\, developed in close collaboration with
  LOOP\n\nMore: http://altspaceloop.com/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantd
 esk&utm_medium=referral
URL:http://altspaceloop.com/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medi
 um=referral
LOCATION:Alternative Space LOOP\, Seoul\, South Korea. Open to individuals 
 and teams across all genres and media\; no separation between artists\, cu
 rators and other applicants.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:acc-individual-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261027T140941Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261110
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261111
SUMMARY:Deadline: Asian Cultural Council Individual Fellowship 2027 (up to 
 USD 35\,000)
DESCRIPTION:The Asian Cultural Council's Individual Fellowship supports sel
 f-directed exploration and cultural exchange experiences in a new or less 
 familiar environment\, fostering exchange between the United States and As
 ia and within Asia. SUPPORT: up to USD 35\,000 for a 1-to-6-month program 
 (minimum 30 days\; two or more months preferred)\; single or joint applica
 tions with up to 3 participants. ELIGIBILITY: citizens or permanent reside
 nts of ACC-eligible countries (the US and countries across Asia\, from Afg
 hanistan to Vietnam)\; age 21+\; at least five years of professional exper
 ience in an eligible discipline (architecture\, archaeology\, art history\
 , arts administration\, arts criticism\, conservation\, crafts\, curation\
 , dance\, ethnomusicology\, film\, literature\, museum studies\, music\, t
 heater\, visual art)\; English proficiency if traveling to the US\; no cou
 rsework or teaching during the fellowship. Grant activities must take plac
 e between 1 August 2027 and 31 December 2028. HOW TO APPLY: via the GoAppl
 y platform\; applications open 1 October 2026. Recommendation letters due 
 16 November 2026. No application fee. DEADLINE: 10 November 2026 at 9:59 A
 M ET.\n\nOrganisation: Asian Cultural Council\nLocation: Self-directed cul
 tural exchange between the US and Asia\, or within Asia. For citizens and 
 permanent residents of ACC-eligible countries (including the US).\nAward: 
 Up to USD 35\,000 for self-directed exploration and cultural exchange of 1
  to 6 months. Single or joint applications (up to 3 participants). No appl
 ication fee.\nDuration: 1 to 6 months (minimum 30 days\; 2 or more months 
 preferred)\, with grant activities between 1 August 2027 and 31 December 2
 028\n\nMore: https://www.asianculturalcouncil.org/grant-opportunities?utm_
 source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.asianculturalcouncil.org/grant-opportunities?utm_source=art
 ificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Self-directed cultural exchange between the US and Asia\, or withi
 n Asia. For citizens and permanent residents of ACC-eligible countries (in
 cluding the US).
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:eulsukdo-busan-land-ecological-art-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261027T140941Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261122
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261123
SUMMARY:Deadline: Busan Metropolitan City: Open Call for Land / Ecological 
 Art on Eulsukdo
DESCRIPTION:Busan Metropolitan City invites individuals and teams from arou
 nd the world to propose a site-responsive work of land and ecological art 
 for Eulsukdo\, an estuarine island at the mouth of the Nakdong River\, Sou
 th Korea's longest river. The island sits in a brackish estuary where rive
 r meets sea and is an important stopover for migratory birds on the East A
 sian-Australasian Flyway\; once heavily altered by agriculture\, landfill 
 and urban development\, it has been restored as wetlands and bird habitat\
 , a layered history the call frames as an invitation to reflect on coexist
 ence between human and more-than-human life. Proposals must respond to Eul
 sukdo's ecology\, landscape\, history and changing environment while minim
 ising ecological disturbance and demonstrating environmental responsibilit
 y across the work's whole lifecycle. ELIGIBILITY: individuals or teams pro
 fessionally active in art\, landscape architecture\, architecture\, design
  or related fields\; interdisciplinary teams welcome. SELECTION: one propo
 sal\, with artwork support of KRW 380\,000\,000 and an artist fee of KRW 2
 0\,000\,000. SUBMISSION WINDOW: 2 November 00:00 to 22 November 2026 24:00
  KST (UTC+9). Provisional opening of the realised work May to June 2027. F
 ull guidelines via the official website (eulsukdo-ecoart.com)\; inquiries 
 to eulsukdo.ecoart@gmail.com.\n\nOrganisation: Busan Metropolitan City\, U
 rban Parks Division\nLocation: Eulsukdo\, Busan\, South Korea (site at 114
 2-8 Hadan-dong\, Saha-gu). Open to individuals and teams worldwide\; the c
 ommissioned work is realised on site.\nAward: One proposal selected. Artwo
 rk support KRW 380\,000\,000 (approx. EUR 235\,000) plus artist fee KRW 20
 \,000\,000 (approx. EUR 12\,500). No entry fee indicated.\nDuration: Singl
 e commission\; provisional opening May to June 2027.\n\nMore: https://euls
 ukdo-ecoart.com/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://eulsukdo-ecoart.com/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_
 medium=referral
LOCATION:Eulsukdo\, Busan\, South Korea (site at 1142-8 Hadan-dong\, Saha-g
 u). Open to individuals and teams worldwide\; the commissioned work is rea
 lised on site.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:acc-organization-grant-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261027T140941Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261110
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261111
SUMMARY:Deadline: Asian Cultural Council Organization Grant 2027 (up to USD
  50\,000)
DESCRIPTION:The Asian Cultural Council's Organization Grant funds arts and 
 culture organisations to send participants on cultural exchange between th
 e US and Asia or within Asia. SUPPORT: up to USD 50\,000. ELIGIBILITY: non
 profit organisations\, government-affiliated or sponsored arts and culture
  institutions\, or academic institutions\, legally registered and based in
  an ACC-eligible location (the US and countries across Asia) with at least
  five years of artistic or cultural programming. The activity lead and all
  participants must be citizens or legal permanent residents of an eligible
  location\, though not necessarily the organisation's own. Eligible discip
 lines: architecture\, archaeology\, art history\, arts administration\, ar
 ts criticism\, conservation\, crafts\, curation\, dance\, ethnomusicology\
 , film\, literature\, museum studies\, music\, theater\, visual art. ACC d
 oes not sponsor visas. Applicants may apply to only one ACC programme per 
 cycle. HOW TO APPLY: via the GoApply portal with an Organization Account (
 https://goapply2.akoyago.com/acc/application/ORG2027). Recommendation lett
 ers due 16 November 2026\, 9:59 AM ET. Questions: the ACC office matching 
 your location (Hong Kong\, Japan\, Philippines and Taiwan have their own o
 ffices\; all others application@accny.org). DEADLINE: 10 November 2026\, 9
 :59 AM ET.\n\nOrganisation: Asian Cultural Council\nLocation: Cultural exc
 hange travel between the US and Asia\, or within Asia. For nonprofit\, gov
 ernment-affiliated or academic organisations registered in ACC-eligible lo
 cations.\nAward: Up to USD 50\,000 per organisation for exchange travel by
  its participants. No application fee.\nDuration: 1 to 6 months of travel 
 (at least 30 consecutive days in the US\, or 30 cumulative days in Asia)\,
  with activities between 1 August 2027 and 31 December 2028\n\nMore: https
 ://www.asianculturalcouncil.org/grant-opportunities?utm_source=artificialn
 ouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.asianculturalcouncil.org/grant-opportunities?utm_source=art
 ificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Cultural exchange travel between the US and Asia\, or within Asia.
  For nonprofit\, government-affiliated or academic organisations registere
 d in ACC-eligible locations.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:daiwa-foundation-small-grants-march-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261027T140941Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20270331
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20270401
SUMMARY:Deadline: Daiwa Foundation Small Grants\, March 2027 round (GBP 2\,
 000-9\,000 for UK-Japan projects)
DESCRIPTION:The Daiwa Foundation Small Grants support projects that promote
  interaction between the UK and Japan in any field\, including arts exchan
 ges\, exhibitions\, conferences\, grassroots and educational exchanges and
  research travel\, with travel between the two countries as the main focus
 . New initiatives are encouraged\, and gender equality and SDG themes are 
 welcome\; continuing or regular events (such as an annual festival edition
 ) are unlikely to be funded without a strong reason or special occasion. N
 OT ELIGIBLE: commissions of artworks\, capital costs\, consumables\, salar
 ies or professional fees\, tuition and commercial activity. WHAT YOU GET: 
 GBP 2\,000 to 9\,000 (most awards GBP 3\,000-4\,000)\, with flights funded
  up to GBP 1\,200 each and subsistence up to GBP 100 per person per day\, 
 plus GBP 50 extra per flight for environmental projects in Japan. APPLICAT
 ION: read the Application Notes\, then apply via the Foundation's online p
 ortal (separate forms for UK- and Japan-based applicants)\; advice from gr
 ants@dajf.org.uk. Decisions by late May or early June 2027. DEADLINE: 31 M
 arch 2027 (the following round closes 30 September).\n\nOrganisation: Daiw
 a Anglo-Japanese Foundation\nLocation: For individuals\, societies\, assoc
 iations and other bodies in the UK or Japan running projects that connect 
 the two countries.\nAward: GBP 2\,000 to 9\,000 (typical awards GBP 3\,000
 -4\,000)\; flights up to GBP 1\,200 each and subsistence up to GBP 100 per
  person per day. A contribution\, not full funding. No application fee.\nD
 uration: Per project\; activity can start from late May or early June 2027
